---
/**
 * /blog/baby-name-app-like-tinder
 *
 * Target: the "baby name app like tinder" family — 9 autocomplete suggestions
 * of their own (verified 2026-08-19, hl=en&gl=us), plus "baby name app swipe"
 * and "baby name app tinder" from the head seed. English twin of the German
 * Tinder page, written natively, not translated. Same evidence rules.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DisclosureEn from '../../components/DisclosureEn.astro';
import WhyNamore from '../../components/WhyNamore.astro';
import ClusterFootEn from '../../components/ClusterFootEn.astro';
import { APPS_EN, VERIFIED_EN, NO_STATEMENT_EN, APP_STORE_EN, PLAY_EN } from '../../data/apps-en';

const canonical = 'https://www.namore.app/blog/baby-name-app-like-tinder';
const campaign = 'app_like_tinder';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is there a baby name app like Tinder?',
    a: 'Several. BabyName, Baby Name Together, Baby Name Genius, Nameberry, BabyCenter, NameHatch and Namore all work with swiping: right if you like a name, left if you do not, and a match when you both said yes. The comparison with Tinder holds up to the match; after that the apps differ a lot in what they do with your matches.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the best baby name swipe app?',
    a: 'It depends on what you need after the swiping. If you just want to get through a lot of names, almost any of them works. If you need to end up with one name, look at what happens after the match. In Namore a final round follows, where you each rate the mutual matches again separately until an order emerges.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does my baby name app keep showing the same names?',
    a: 'Because many apps stop reliably excluding already-rated names once there are a lot of them. It is the most common complaint about swipe apps. In Namore a name you swiped away does not come back, not after a thousand swipes and not after weeks. It was the bug that cost us the most work.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can couples swipe baby names together?',
    a: 'Yes, that is what these apps are built for. How you connect differs: BabyName describes pairing by shareable link or AirDrop, Baby Name Together links accounts through email, BabyCenter uses personal codes. In Namore one of you opens a session and the other joins with a six-letter code, with no account.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'Baby name app like Tinder: what swiping actually solves',
      description:
        'Swiping baby names like Tinder: which apps do it, where the comparison stops working, and what matters after the match.',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      datePublished: '2026-08-20',
      dateModified: '2026-08-20',
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Julian Ziegler' },
      publisher: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'Namore',
        logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://www.namore.app/logo@2x.png' },
      },
      mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': canonical },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Baby name app like Tinder: what swiping actually solves | Namore"
  description="Swiping baby names like Tinder: which apps do it, where the comparison stops working, and why what happens after the match decides everything."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="post">
    <DisclosureEn />

    <header class="post-head">
      <p class="post-kicker">Guide · Retrieved {VERIFIED_EN}</p>
      <h1>Baby name app like Tinder</h1>
    </header>

    <p class="post-standfirst">
      Yes, there are several. Right if you like the name, left if you do not, and when you both
      swiped right on the same name, it is a match. The principle works surprisingly well, right
      up to the point where the Tinder comparison stops carrying.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the Tinder comparison ends</h2>
    <p>
      On Tinder, the match is the goal. You found each other; life takes over from there. With
      names, the match is only the shortlist. At some point we had fourteen mutual matches and
      stood exactly where we started, just with a shorter list to argue about.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the most useful question to ask a swipe app is not how nicely it swipes, but what happens
      afterwards. And the second, underrated one: whether a name you swiped away actually stays
      away. In many apps it resurfaces after a few hundred swipes, and that is the moment couples
      stop trusting the app.
    </p>

    <h2>Which apps swipe</h2>
    <p>Here is what the vendors themselves say, typed out and linked, so you do not have to take our word for it.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="cmp">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>App</th><th>What the vendor says about swiping together</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="is-ours">
            <td data-label="App">Namore <span class="sf-note">(our app)</span></td>
            <td data-label="What the vendor says">You swipe separately; a name only appears as a match once you have both said yes independently. A final round follows. <span class="sf-note">Our description, not quoted from a listing.</span></td>
          </tr>
          {APPS_EN.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="App"><a href={a.src} rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{a.name}</a></td>
              <td data-label="What the vendor says">{a.partner ? <q>{a.partner.q}</q> : <span class="sf-none">{NO_STATEMENT_EN}</span>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Pick any swipe app if …</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>you are at the start and just want to see a lot of names.</li>
      <li>you want to try the principle first; all of these are free to download.</li>
      <li>database size matters more to you than what follows.</li>
      <li>you are searching alone and do not need the couples part at all.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Pick Namore if …</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>you have already swiped plenty and still have no decision.</li>
      <li>it bothers you when names you swiped away come back.</li>
      <li>you do not want ads between the names.</li>
      <li>you do not want to register anywhere.</li>
    </ul>

    <WhyNamore
      lang="en"
      heading="What happens after the swiping, in our app"
      only={['design', 'decision-round', 'no-repeats', 'search-add', 'no-ads']}
    />

    <p class="post-caveat">
      Where Namore is weaker: it is new, with few ratings. If you want the reassurance of a long
      established app, the bigger ones above are the safer pick.
    </p>

    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-ink" href={APP_STORE_EN(campaign)}>Get Namore for iOS</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={PLAY_EN(campaign)}>Get it on Google Play</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <div class="faq-item">
          <dt>{f.q}</dt>
          <dd>{f.a}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <ClusterFootEn current="baby-name-app-like-tinder" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
